- First calculate the height of the altitude by letting the height of the triangle $BCD$.
- Make sure that the length of $\overline{AD}=6-BD=6-(5-CE)=CE+5$.
- Then use the similarity of $\triangle{DCE}$ and $\triangle{ABC}$ to get $BD$.

Step 2: Use the similarity of $\triangle{DCE}$ and $\triangle{ABC}$ to find $BD$:
$\triangle{DCE} \sim \triangle{ABC}\Rightarrow \frac{AC}{CE} = \frac{BD}{AB}$

\begin{align*}
\frac{AC}{CE} &= \frac{BD}{AB} \\
\frac{6}{CE} &= \frac{BD}{5} \\
BD &= \frac{5\times 6}{CE}= \boxed{\tfrac{30}{CE}}
\end{align*}

- Now we have $BD=\frac{30}{CE}$.

Step 3: Calculate the length of $\overline{DE}$ using Pythagoras:

\begin{align*}
DE^2 &= AC^2 - CE^2 & \text{ (since $DE$ is the hypotenuse)} \\
DE^2 &= (5+5)^2 - CE^2 & \text{ (since $AC=10$)} \\
DE^2 &= 100 - CE^2
\end{align*}

Square the difference between $BD$ and $\frac{30}{CE}$ to eliminate the fraction and get an expression quadratic in $CE$.

\begin{align*}
(BD)^2 - CE^2 &= (30-BD)^2\\
\left(\tfrac{30}{CE}\right)^2 - CE^2 &= (30-BD)^2\\
\tfrac{900}{CE^2} - CE^2 &= 900 - 60(BD-30)\\
0 &= CE^2( CE - \sqrt{CE^2 + 3600}) - 5400
\end{align*}

- Expand the quadratic and solve for $CE$. Since $CE$ is positive, consider the positive root of the quadratic equation.

Step 4: Calculate the length of $\overline{DE}$:

\begin{align*}
CE &= \sqrt{CE^2 + 3600} - \sqrt{5400} &\text{ positive root of Step 3 equation} \\
CE &= \sqrt{DE^2 + 900} - \sqrt{5400} &\text{ substituting the expression of DE from Step 2} \\
DE^2 &= (CE + \sqrt{5400})^2 - 900
\end{align*}

Now, since $DE$ is the hypotenuse of the right triangle $\triangle{DEC}$:

\begin{align*}
DEC &= \frac{1}{2} \times DE \times CE \\
DEC &= \frac{1}{2} \times [(CE + \sqrt{5400})^2 - 6^2] \\
DEC &= \boxed{[(\tfrac{CE}{2}+\sqrt{5400})^2-\tfrac{36}{2}]}, \boxed{<simplest_value_in_simplified_form}
\end{align*}

