Let's investigate the parity of even functions' sum and difference to find the parity of $f(x) \pm g(x)$. Since $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are even functions, for $n\in \mathbb{Z}$, $f(-n) = f(n)$ and $g(-n) = g(n)$.

We'll discuss the three simple cases $f(x) \pm g(x)$ for $n\in \mathbb{Z}$:

1. $f(x) + g(x)$: \begin{align*}
   f(-n) + g(-n) &= f(n) + g(n) \\
   f(-n) + g(-n) &= f(n) + f(n) \quad \text{ (Since $g(x) = f(x)$)} \\
   f(-n) + g(-n) &= 2f(n) \quad \text{ (Since $f(x)$ is an even function)}
 \end{align*}
For an even number $n$, we have $2f(n) = n_1$, where $n_1$ is an even number. For an odd number $n$, we have $2f(n) = n_2$, where $n_2 = 2f(\frac{n}{2}) + 2f(\frac{n}{2})$ is a sum of two odd numbers and thus an even number. Therefore,  $f(x) + g(x)$ is even.

2. $f(x) - g(x)$: \begin{align*}
   f(-n) - g(-n) &= f(n) - g(n) \\
   f(-n) - g(-n) &= f(n) - f(n) \quad \text{ (Since $g(x) = f(x)$)} \\
   f(-n) - g(-n) &= 0
 \end{align*}
So, $f(x) - g(x)$ is always an even number, specifically $0$.

Since the difference of two functions is always smaller than their sum in magnitude, we have $|f(x) + g(x)| \leq |f(x) - g(x)|$. The function $f(x) + g(x)$ is even and positive for all real numbers since it's a sum of two even functions, whose values are positive or zero. Thus, we can reasonably guess that $f(x) - g(x)$ is zero except for a finite number of cases when $f(x) > g(x)$, such as $f(0) > g(0)$ or $f(1) > g(1)$. In the unlikely scenario that $f(x) - g(x) \neq 0$, it will be an odd function, but we can discard this possibility since $|f(x) - g(x)| = 0$ most of the time.

Now that we have analyzed both $f(x) + g(x)$ and $f(x) - g(x)$, we can conclude that the sum of two even functions forms an even function except possibly at finitely many points, while the difference is always an even number, specifically zero. Therefore, the sum of two even functions is an even function.

\boxed{ even }

