To find the lateral surface area of a cylinder, we use the formula $2\pi r(h)$, where $r$ is the radius and $h$ is the height. We must first find the radius, which is half the diameter, in this case:

\begin{align}
r & = \frac{d}{2} \\
& = \frac{12}{2} \\
& = \boxed{ 6 } \text{ square centimeters }
\end{align}

Now we can compute the lateral surface area:

\begin{align}
A_L & = 2\pi r\cdot h \\
& = 2\cdot\pi\cdot6\cdot6 \\
& = (6\pi)(6) \\
& = \boxed{ 36\pi } \text{ square centimeters }
\end{align}

To find the volume, we use the formula $\pi r^2 h$:

\begin{align}
V & = \pi r^2 h \\
& = \pi(6^2)(6) \\
& = (36\pi)(6) \\
& = \boxed{ 216\pi } \text{ cubic centimeters }
\end{align}

