\mathbf{Step 1:} Find the solutions of the equation $\cos{n}=-\cos{568}.$

\because the cosine function is periodic with a period of $360^\circ,$ (or $2\pi$ radians) we can focus on the range $0^\circ\le n\le360^\circ$ (or $0\le n\le2\pi$).

Solving $\cos{n}=\cos{568}$ directly may be challenging, but we know that $\cos{568}=\cos{(-180+568)}=\cos{748},$ and $\cos{n}=\cos{n+180k}$ for any integer $k.$

We'll try values of $n$ such that $738\le n\le748$ (in increments of $2^\circ$) since $\cos{n}$ is decreasing on this interval as $n$ increases.

\begin{align*}
&\cos{738^\circ} \approx 0.02401684795907 \dots\\
&\cos{740^\circ} \approx -0.02403131193721 \dots\\
&\cos{742^\circ} \approx -0.02404557253328 \dots\\
&\cos{744^\circ} \approx -0.02405973819967 \dots\\
&\cos{746^\circ} \approx -0.02407380991605 \dots\\
\Leftrightarrow n&=740^\circ
\end{align*}

\mathbf{Step 2:} Convert $740^\circ$ from degrees to radians.

\begin{align*}
\text{1 degree} &= \frac{2\pi}{360},\;\; \therefore\;\; \text{180 degrees} &= \frac{2\pi\cdot180}{360} = \pi \\
\text{740}^\circ &= \frac{740}{360}\cdot\pi = \boxed{2\cdot\pi} \text{ radians}
\end{align*}

