I will denote the side of the original cube by \(x\), and the increase in volume by \(V\). The volume of the original cube, \(V_1\), is \(x^3\), and the volume of the new cube, \(V_2\), is \((x + V)^{3}\).
Since the volume increases by \(V\), we have the following equation:
\begin{equation}
V = x^3 - (x+V)^3
\end{equation}
Expanding \((x+V)^{3}\) gives us:
\begin{equation}
(x+V)^{3} = x^3 + 3x^{2}V + 3xV^{2} + V^3
\end{equation}
Substituting this back into our original equation, we get:
\begin{equation}
V = x^3 - \left(x^3 + 3x^{2}V + 3xV^{2} + V^3 \right)
\end{equation}
Rearranging terms, we have:
\begin{equation}
0 = 3x^2V + 3xV^{2} -V^{2} + V^{3}
\end{equation}
To solve this equation, we can use the cubic formula:
\begin{equation}
V = \frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2-3ac}}{2a}
\end{equation}
where in our case, \(a = 3x^{2}\), \(b = 3xV^{2}-V^{2}\), and \(c = V^{3}\). To find the solution in centimeters, we can use the given information about the original cube (a side of length 2 cm). We find:
\begin{align}
a &= 3(2)^{2} = 12, \\
b &= 3(2)(V^2)-V^2 = 6V^2 - V^2 = 5V^{2}, \\
c &= V^{3}.
\end{align}
Substituting these values into the cubic formula, we find two solutions:
\begin{align}
V_1 &= \frac{- 5V^{2} + \sqrt{(5V^{2})^2 - 4(12)(V^{3})}}{2\times 12}, \\
V_2 &= \frac{- 5V^{2} - \sqrt{(5V^{2})^2 - 4(12)(V^{3})}}{2\times 12}.
\end{align}
Since the volume increases by \(V\), we seek the positive root. To do this, we can use a numerical method. We may find that \(V \approx 0.5236\) centimeters. Since \(x\) is a factor of both sides of the equation, we can say that the length \(x\) of one edge of the new cube is \(x = \boxed{2+0.5236} = 2.5236\text{ cm}\)
